<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Trebia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/218-bce">218 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a> was fought in 218 BCE in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the first major victory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> after his crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. A Roman army was lured across the river and caught in an ambush. The battle is a celebrated example of Carthaginian generalship in the military history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trebia.jpg" alt="Battle of the Trebia" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthäus Merian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Trebbia_(M._Merian)_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Second Punic War followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> of 264 to 241 BCE, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Hamilcar Barca then expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthaginian-iberia">Carthaginian Iberia</a> into a quasi-monarchical territory ruled by his family, the Barcids, which gave Carthage silver mines, manpower and the means to resist Roman demands. In 218 BCE Hannibal led an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, reaching northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in October with 38,000 infantry and 8,000 cavalry.</p><p>The Gallic tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> had risen against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> after colonists were settled at Placentia and Cremona. At the Battle of Ticinus, Publius Scipio led the Roman cavalry and light infantry against the Carthaginian cavalry, was beaten and wounded, and the Romans withdrew to a fortified camp near Placentia. The Senate ordered the second consul, Sempronius Longus, whose army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> had been meant for an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, to march north. Because Scipio was partly incapacitated, Sempronius took overall command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After a day of heavy skirmishing in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> gained the upper hand, Sempronius was eager for battle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> had his brother Mago take 1,000 cavalry and 1,000 infantry in the night to hide in an old brush-filled watercourse beside the flood plain where he meant to fight. His Numidian cavalry then lured the Romans out of their camp onto ground of his choosing. The armies were each about 40,000 strong, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> stronger in cavalry and the Romans in infantry.</p><p>Fresh Carthaginian cavalry routed the outnumbered Roman cavalry, and light infantry outflanked the Roman infantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> had also placed his elephants on either side of the infantry to discourage the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> from approaching the flanks, and he thinned his line, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> in the centre, to match the Roman length. The hidden force then struck the Roman infantry in the rear. Most Roman units collapsed, but about 10,000 under Sempronius kept formation and fought their way out to Placentia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Historians disagree about Roman losses. Paul Erdkamp puts them at 20,000 killed, half the force, not counting prisoners. Carthaginian losses were several thousand, mostly Gallic infantry in the centre, and several elephants. News of the defeat caused panic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at first, but this calmed when Sempronius arrived to preside over the consular elections. Gnaeus Servilius Geminus and Gaius Flaminius were chosen, and the new consuls raised fresh legions and built a fleet of 60 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quinquereme">quinqueremes</a>.</p><p>Gallic recruits flocked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, whose army grew to 60,000. In spring 217 BCE he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennine Mountains</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-trasimene">Battle of Lake Trasimene</a>, and in 216 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a>, the last of what modern historians call the three great military calamities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in the first three years of the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Trebia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Three crushing Roman defeats followed: the <b>Trebia</b> in 218 BCE, Lake Trasimene in 217 BCE and above all Cannae in 216 BCE, where Hannibal’s double envelopment of a Roman army of perhaps 80,000 men killed an estimated 47,000–70,000 in a single afternoon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second Punic War Hannibal destroyed Roman armies at the <b>Trebia</b>, Lake Trasimene and Cannae in 218–216 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li></ul></section>
